Some spoily food/ hunger advce. One or two of these might be a bit
slashemy:

(A) Don't feed your pet unless he is obviously hungry or you are trying
to train him. He can look after himself most of the time. If he keeps
getting all the food before you do, zap him with a sleep ray or shut
him in a room. If you encounter a floating eye, wait for the pet to
freeze itself before killing it- then you can be sure to get first dibs
on the corpse. You can also eat pets if you get hungry enough- only
dogs and cats will incur any kind of penalty.

(B) Eat every (non poisonous, non domestic, non-cannibalistic,
non-hallucinogenic, non-acidic, non-stunning, fresh=-) corspe you can
get your hands on. The dungeon won't usually grant you enough of these
on early levels to sustain you indefinitely, but it will help a lot.
Figurines and wands/spells of create monster can supplement this part
of your diet.

(C) Get poison resistance, it really broadens your culinary horizons.

(D) When you get to "weak" or "fainting", you can pray to fill your
stomach. Just remember that you can't pray too often. Either keep track
of your prayer timeout by watching your turn counter and reading a
spoiler, or just sacrifice stuff (assuming you have an handy altar).
Sometimes it's better, hunger-wise, to sacrifice that corpse to appease
your god then pray for sustenance than it is to just eat it. Also be
aware that a hunger-prayer can also be a holy-water-prayer, so try to
make the most of each prayer.

(E) If you can cast stone to flesh successfully, you can turn rocks
into snacks.

(F) If you have a wand of poly, you can use it to turn light snacks or
inedible corpses into proper meals.

(G) Buy food from shops. Do this before you get hungry, because
shopkeeps will take advantage shamelessly. Better yet, steal=-)

(H) Beware of eggs. If rotten, they will make you vomit, which makes
you hungrier. There's also a risk of it being a cockatrive egg. I
usually let the pet have them.

(I) While it is usually a bad idea to eat tripe, bats, acidic corpses
and yellow/ purple molds, in desperate situations they are often better
than nothing. Contrary to common sense, there's no absolutely reason
not to eat sewer rats.

(J) You can pretty much stop worrying about food when you get to
sokoban. In the meantime though, the mines are stuffed full of
delicious dwarves and gnutritious gnomes.

(K) SCMIIW, but I think some potions have a little nutritional value,
namely water & fruit juice. Quaffing from fountains will reduce your
hunger only some of the time.

(I) I know I'm gonna get in trouble for this one, but I advise hungry
players to kick sinks: They cough up black puddings, which can be
dangerous but are full of vitamins, minerals, proteins, carbohydrates,
marrowbone jelly, pro-vitamin B5, harmful acids and yummy intrisics,
and can be 'farmed' for a more or less infinite supply of food. (look
up 'pudding farming' in  a spoiler for more info). Sinks also yield
rings, which may or may not be slow digestion. There are risks of
course, but this is nethack.

(J) No matter how paranoid you are about starving, don't eat while
satiated. Just don't do it.

(K) If you have the means, poly yourself into a rock mole or umber
hulk, and then you can eat dirt! Other monsters can make a meal of
armour, weapons, and all kinds of other junk. Alternatively, turn ought
to be yourself into an egg laying monster (like a female dragon), lay
some eggs and eat them. I think there may be a penalty of some kind for
doing this, but it beats starvation. IMHO the fact that it costs no
nutrition to lay an egg is a bit of a bug.

(L) Some things make you hungry more quickly. These include wearing
rings (with one obvious exception), wearing amulets, digging, carrying
too much gear and pushing boulders.

(M) There are many ways to locate food: There's food detection,
obviously, but object detection will work just as well. Sometimes there
will be a food ration lurking in a walled up or secret cupboard that
you would otherwise have missed. Similarly, telepathy and other
monster-detection tricks might find you a tasty pack of rothes or hill
orcs in an unexplored corridor, room, cupboard or vault.

(N) An early wish spent on a =oSD is not necessarily a wish wasted,
imho.


